{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ujz6enRLor676DF2PHpfm7","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Xu Mavrikakis 2001 O adsorption Cu(111) binding energy fcc site eV atomic oxygen DFT\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Quantum Many-Body Simulations of Catalytic Metal Surfaces\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.13036\"},{\"title\":\"Embedded quantum computing for many-body surface reaction\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2607.27009\"},{\"title\":\"unibo.it\",\"url\":\"https://acnpsearch.tweb-dev.unibo.it/singlejournalindex/86725655\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2505.12914\"},{\"title\":\"Structural and electronic properties of atomic oxygen adsorption on Cu(111) surface: A first-principles investigation\",\"url\":\"https://bvbvf1.bib-bvb.de/vufind/PrimoRecord/cdi_chongqing_primary_44769476\"},{\"title\":\"s11433 012 4868 5\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s11433-012-4868-5.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Adsorption and dissociation of O2 on Cu(111):: thermochemistry, reaction barrier and the effect of strain\",\"url\":\"https://data.zhenzhi.com/details/1/867115130\"},{\"title\":\"par.nsf.gov\",\"url\":\"https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10018902\"},{\"title\":\"Appl Cat A 172 85 1998\",\"url\":\"https://nomad.fhi.mpg.de/uploads/publications/th/Appl-Cat-A-172-85-1998.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"The adsorption and dissociation of O2 molecular precursors on Cu:: the effect of steps\",\"url\":\"https://data.zhenzhi.com/details/1/888079892\"}]\n\nThe search turned up the abstract for the 2001 paper, but not its full text, so I could not confirm the exact fcc-versus-hcp breakdown.\n\n- **Source paper:** Xu and Mavrikakis, \"Adsorption and dissociation of O2 on Cu(111): thermochemistry, reaction barrier and the effect of strain,\" *Surface Science* 494(2), 2001, pp. 131–144 (Y. Xu et M. Mavrikakis, Adsorption and dissociation of O-2 on Cu(111): thermochemistry, reaction barrier and the effect of strain, SURF SCI, 494(2), 2001, pp. 131-144).\n- **Atomic O site and energy:** The abstract reports that atomic oxygen prefers threefold hollow sites, with a binding energy of about -4.3 eV relative to a gas-phase O atom (Atomic oxygen is found to prefer threefold hollows. with a binding energy of ca. -4.3 eV with respect to a gas-phase O atom.). The abstract does not say whether that site is fcc or hcp.\n- **Other results:** A separate Cu(211) step study cites the same -4.3 eV value for atomic O on Cu(111) (the binding energy of atomic oxygen is -4.5 eV at its preferred site on the relaxed Cu(211) surface vs. -4.3 eV at its preferred site on the relaxed Cu(111) surface). A more recent DFT study reports fcc at -5.000 eV and hcp at -4.905 eV, but that is a different calculation, so its numbers are not directly comparable to the 2001 value (Oxygen (O*) fcc -5.000 hcp -4.905).\n\nTo confirm the fcc/hcp split in the 2001 paper, check the full text in *Surface Science*.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"68e3c0ea-1e39-4c13-9e54-6a3ef38db9e4","uuid":"7a06bf2d-7105-4994-818c-873a04571710","timestamp":"2026-11-01T07:33:00.278Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Xu Mavrikakis 2001 O adsorption Cu(111) binding energy fcc site eV atomic oxygen DFT","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_015S9x2kCa1DYkcAigw7TXM8","content":[{"title":"Quantum Many-Body Simulations of Catalytic Metal Surfaces","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.13036"},{"title":"Embedded quantum computing for many-body surface reaction","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2607.27009"},{"title":"unibo.it","url":"https://acnpsearch.tweb-dev.unibo.it/singlejournalindex/86725655"},{"title":"arxiv.org","url":"https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2505.12914"},{"title":"Structural and electronic properties of atomic oxygen adsorption on Cu(111) surface: A first-principles investigation","url":"https://bvbvf1.bib-b